// TLOU RT - ray tracing on The Last of Us Part II Remastered's own G-buffer (D3D12), injected into the game's lighting.
//
// Where it runs: CS_DeferredLighting (the game's tiled deferred lighting: 44 ExecuteIndirect permutations recorded in one
// command list on the direct queue) leaves the light buffer in the UAV state and the game's lighting descriptor tables
// bound when its command list ends. At that list's close_command_list event (raised before the game's Close) the add-on
// records its passes: it sets its own root signature - a copy of the game's lighting root signature (rebuilt from the
// description ReShade reports at init_pipeline_layout; the game's shaders do not embed it) with the add-on's root
// descriptors and 8 root constants added after the game's parameters - re-binds the game's descriptor tables / root
// descriptors recorded on that list at the same indices, and dispatches its compute passes with UAV barriers in between.
// If anything else (a draw, another dispatch, a texture state change, another compute root signature) is recorded after
// the lighting in that list, nothing is injected for that list. Shader inputs and passes: see src\rt.hlsl. The light
// buffer then goes on to the game's forward pass, fog, transparency, particles and DLSS.
//
// Scene geometry for hardware ray tracing: the visibility prepass (pipelines whose only render target is RG32_UINT:
// MainVsDepthOnly + MainPsDepthOnly*, 7 command lists before the lighting on the same queue, ~1800-1900 draws / 3.1-3.4M
// triangles at the test spot) draws the visible scene. For each of its draws the add-on inserts the same draw right before
// it with a clone of the game's pipeline that streams the clip-space xyz out (no rasterisation). Stream output needs a
// root signature created with ALLOW_STREAM_OUTPUT, which the game's lack, so the clone uses a native copy of the game's
// root signature with that flag: the add-on switches to it, re-binds the game's root arguments recorded on that list,
// draws, then switches back, re-binds them again and restores the game's pipeline. Every draw streams into its own part of
// the current frame region with its own filled-size counter (consecutive draws sharing a counter overwrite each other: v6).
// The frame region alternates at each lighting list; the lighting injection converts the frame's region to world
// positions in place, refits / builds one BLAS per draw (v9), builds the TLAS, traces the bounce rays with RayQuery and
// checks the scene with camera rays (overlay status lines, with GPU and CPU timings).
//
// v1: injection test (debug view). v2: ray traced bounce light (screen-space rays on the real G-buffer, temporal
// accumulation, edge-aware filter); sliders in the ReShade overlay (Add-ons tab), saved in ReShade.ini [TLOU RT].
// v3: fixes v2's crash (access violation in on_push_descriptors: root SRVs/UAVs arrive as raw GPU addresses).
// v4-v7: geometry capture on Numpad - (stream-out of the visibility prepass, written to rt-recon\geo-<time>\) to check the
//     captured triangles offline against the depth buffer (geo_check.py). v7 result (geo-20261010-082521): all draws
//     complete, 100% of the depth buffer covered, 75.8% on it (median 3.2e-6 relative), 0% behind, 24.2% in front
//     (alpha-tested cut-outs). Source of the capture: src\tlou_rt_v7.cpp.bak.
// v8: hardware ray tracing every frame: one BLAS (fast build) over the frame's region + a one-instance TLAS. Result (user,
//     2026-10-10): 3.13M triangles from 1789 draws, camera-ray check 77.2% on the surface / 22.8% in front / 0% missed,
//     but the BLAS build took 6.42 ms per frame (trace 0.13 ms). Source: src\tlou_rt_v8.cpp.bak, src\rt_v8.hlsl.bak.
// v9: one BLAS per draw, kept between frames and refitted (Remix-style), absolute world space, TLAS with one instance per
//     draw (see "per-draw BLAS cache"). Result (user, 2026-10-10): BLAS 1.10 ms (21 built, 1964 refit) + TLAS 0.16 ms
//     instead of 6.42 ms, 3.40M triangles, camera-ray check 70.4% on the surface / 29.6% in front / 0% missed, CPU 3.86 ms;
//     frame rate barely changes with the add-on on or off.
// v10: recon for replacing the game's lighting inputs (Remix-style path tracing): descriptor tracking names the textures
//     behind CS_DeferredLighting's descriptor tables, and Numpad - captures everything the lighting reads (through its
//     own bindings) into rt-recon\inputs-<time>\ (manifest.txt + dump.bin, decode_inputs.py). Result
//     (inputs-20261010-100457): t4 / t5 = G-buffer render targets 4 / 5 (R11G11B10_FLOAT ambient irradiance, baked
//     lightmap quality), t10 = RGBA8 (x sun shadow mask, y ambient occlusion), t18 = R11G11B10 reflections, t19 = 32x32
//     split-sum LUT, one light (t14 = 436 bytes) with a 1536^2 shadow map; the sun colour c[7] was 0 there (overcast).
//     But descriptor copy tracking cost 127 ms of CPU per frame (82,624 descriptors per frame): removed again in v11.
// v11: Remix-style path-traced indirect light before the game's lighting: at the first CS_DeferredLighting dispatch of
//     the lighting list (the game's root signature, tables and pipeline bound) the add-on builds the frame's geometry,
//     path traces the near field (rt.hlsl) and copies the rewritten ambient inputs into G-buffer targets 4 and 5, then
//     restores the game's compute state (root signature, tables, root descriptors, root constants, pipeline) before the
//     game's ExecuteIndirect. After the lighting (list close) cs_history keeps the lit result for the next frame's hits.
//     The old additive composite after the lighting is gone. Sliders: bounce light, game ambient, ray length, occlusion
//     (new ini keys: their meaning changed).
// v12: fixes v11's crash on loading a level (access violation in the NVIDIA driver, crash dump 2026-10-10 10:27: a
//     stream-out draw re-bound its tables onto a root signature that was not valid any more). The game records its lists
//     in fiber jobs that can move between threads, and v8-v11 kept the recording state per thread: the state now lives
//     with each command list. Also: cached copies of a root signature the game destroys are retired (a new one can get its
//     address), destroyed G-buffer targets are forgotten, and overlay line 4 counts the lighting lists whose ambient was
//     rewritten (and why not). Result (user, 2026-10-10): no crash; with Game ambient 0 and bounce 12.83 (ini) the sky
//     light was gone, so occlusion had nothing to darken, and faces were lit only by their own hair (noisy, tinted).
// v13: path-traced sky shadows. The game's ambient is a flat part (t4) plus a lobe from a per-pixel dominant direction
//     (t5, direction in G-buffer 1 .z: mostly the open sky, 72% of the ambient): shadow rays toward it within a cone
//     (Shadow softness slider) give that lobe's visibility, the cosine rays the flat part's (rt.hlsl). 4 cosine + 2
//     shadow rays per half-resolution pixel, an SVGF-style filter (5 passes, variance-guided), hair strands left out of
//     the rays (MainPsDepthOnlyObjIdHairDither draws: instance mask), a darker guess for hits the camera did not see.
//     "Game ambient" is now "Sky light" (new ini key).
// v14: scene data for a Remix-style path tracer (no visible change yet). A material cache: Remix knows the material of
//     every triangle a ray hits, here cs_material learns it from the camera's view (a camera ray per sampled pixel names
//     the triangle under it, the G-buffer gives its albedo and roughness), one slot per triangle of each cached BLAS in
//     the geo buffer (allocated, cleared and freed with the entry). Triangle normals at hits from the frame's vertices.
//     The lighting's other inputs the path tracer will write: the sun shadow mask (t10) as MainPs_CloudAndAmbientShadow's
//     render target, the reflections (t18, a compute pass's UAV) through the lighting's descriptor table during a
//     descriptor tracking window of a few presents (copies tracked only then: 127 ms of CPU per frame when always on).
//     The Numpad - capture shows the "ray view" (what camera rays hit: cached albedo, normal) and names the inputs.
//     Result (user, 2026-10-10, capture inputs-20261010-142601): the ray view reproduces the scene with per-triangle
//     colours (96% of camera hits learnt, 61% of bounce hits), hit normals right, t4 / t5 / t10 / t18 confirmed through
//     the lighting's descriptors; view-write tracking cost ~8 ms in a frame (switched to windows only by itself).
// v15: the Remix-style integrator on that scene data (rt.hlsl hit_light): a ray's hit gives back what the camera saw
//     there last frame, else the hit triangle's cached material lit by the sun (shadow ray) and one continuation ray.
//     Ray-traced sun shadows into the sun shadow mask (t10.x times the visibility toward the sun: the game's shadow maps
//     keep the casters the game did not draw) and ray-traced reflections into t18 (GGX rays; the game's reflection stays
//     where they hit nothing). The copies into t10 / t18 start from the state their last barrier showed. Sliders: Sun
//     softness (cone of the sun rays), Reflections (share of the path-traced reflections).
//     Result (user, 2026-10-10): no visible reflections, no sun shadows (overcast there), and a gray block over the top-left
//     quarter of the screen: the game's render targets are pooled (G-buffer targets 4 / 5 swap between frames, t10 and t18
//     change), and v15 wrote into the t10 / t18 it had found once, which other frames used for something else.
// v16: the lighting's inputs are resolved every frame through its own descriptors and only those resources are written.
//     ReShade 6.8's encoded descriptor handles make it cheap: every descriptor copy into a CBV/SRV/UAV heap is recorded per
//     destination slot with bit operations, and a slot's texture is the view ReShade registered at its source. Barrier
//     states per resource for the copies (t18 only from a state a barrier showed). The descriptor windows, the view-write
//     tracking and the sun shadow mask's pipeline tag are gone.
//     Result (user, 2026-10-10 15:25): crash after ~50 s (access violation in ReShade's get_resource_desc, called from
//     pre_lighting): a stale slot record led to a view whose texture was destroyed (ReShade keeps views by descriptor
//     address and does not drop them with their resource), and GetDesc ran on freed memory.
// v17: slot records carry the present they were written in (copies, and views written in place: update events with the
//     same bit decoding); the lighting's slots are followed only when written in the last two presents. A resolved
//     resource is accepted only from the add-on's set of live candidate textures (init_resource / destroy_resource:
//     R11G11B10_FLOAT and RGBA8 2D textures), with size and format from that set: no call on an unverified pointer.
//     Result (user, 2026-10-10): works, t4 / t5 / t10 / t18 found and written every frame; descriptor tracking 3.7 ms CPU.
// v18: the game's lights, ray traced (rt.hlsl cs_lights ...). CS_DeferredLighting loops over the lights of each 16x16 tile
//     (t13 = light count per tile, t12 = indices, t14 = descriptions) with shadow maps. At the first lighting dispatch the
//     add-on copies a descriptor of an all-zero count buffer into the lighting table's own t13 slot (the slot is filled
//     from the game's staging descriptors every frame, so this frame only), so the game's loop lights nothing, and binds
//     the real count range as a root SRV for its own passes: no state changes on the game's buffers. After the lighting it
//     evaluates every light as the game does, with ray-traced visibility instead of shadow maps, and adds the result to the
//     light buffer with the lighting's final factor (before cs_history). Sliders: Light shadows (0 = the game's own lights
//     and shadow maps), Light size (smallest light radius for the shadow rays). Descriptor tracking times one call in 64.
// v19: full path tracing (GBRT / Remix-style, the user's direction 2026-10-10: the game's own lighting cannot give
//     ray-traced shadows like that while its baked ambient dominates). After the lighting cs_full lights every surface
//     pixel itself - the game's sun term with ray-traced visibility instead of its shadow maps, the game's lights (v18),
//     path-traced bounce and sky on the diffuse albedo, the reflections through the game's split-sum LUT, skin's
//     subsurface light - and blends it over the game's result (Path tracing slider, 1 = all). Rays that leave the near
//     field take the game's environment probes (Cs_SpecularIbl's 250 cubemaps, chosen per tile and box-projected as it
//     does): its root arguments are taken at its dispatch and bound on a mirror of its parameters (register space 101)
//     in the add-on's root signature, which is rebuilt once Cs_SpecularIbl's root signature is known.
// v20: temporal stability on moving characters (user 2026-10-10: the light "flickers from on to off" in a cutscene; a
//     diff of two frames showed patches on the character switching, the background steady). Every history followed the
//     camera only, so on a character who breathes or talks it was dropped or smeared each frame. cs_motion (after the
//     TLAS) finds the triangle under each pixel and where it was last frame: each BLAS entry remembers the vertex data
//     offset of its draw (last_data, last_epoch), and the table hands the previous epoch's offset to the shader (+28;
//     that region keeps last frame's converted vertices until the next prepass). The temporal passes reproject with it.
//     The lights trace up to 4 shadow rays per pixel (shared among up to 4 lights, else stratified picks). Light
//     shadows 0 with Path tracing above 0 switched the game's light loop back on, and cs_full then blended the game's
//     lights away with the rest of its result: the add-on now lights them unshadowed instead.
// v21 (user 2026-10-10, two Game Bar clips checked frame by frame with a Media Foundation decoder: path tracing floods
//     rooms with light (3.6x brighter than off), leaves and hair flicker, hair glows, faces flat, a dark spot jumps in the
//     middle of Ellie's flashlight beam): rt.hlsl - escaped rays take the baked ambient again (the probes lit rooms through
//     the off-screen ceiling and walls), full path tracing keeps the game's small-scale occlusion and ambient shadow, the
//     half-resolution sample is the front-most pixel of its block, hair keeps the game's lighting (cs_motion flags the
//     pixels whose triangle is a hair draw; motion plane 16 bytes per pixel), the bounce feedback keeps one physical
//     bounce, shadow rays skip what a light's shadow map leaves out near the light (its near plane, decoded from the
//     lighting: depth = depth_a / w + depth_b) and 5% of the distance. Status line: the shadow maps' near planes.
// v22 (user 2026-10-10 clip: Ellie's own body still shadowed her flashlight beam - the game puts that light at her head
//     and leaves the player (stencil bit 64) out of its shadow map; grass cast solid slabs; the small rocks' hard shadows
//     shimmered; a companion's hair glowed in the beam): lights with a game shadow map use it, read exactly as the
//     lighting reads it (rt.hlsl game_shadow_map: tetrahedral point maps, projected spot / box maps, the PCSS-style filter,
//     contrast, minimum, fade, cavity, screen shadow), and get no rays; lights without one get the game's G-buffer
//     occlusion and the rays. Cut-out cards - prepass pixel shaders that bind a sampler (alpha test: 77 of the 79
//     discarding variants of the recon dump; PSV0 resource table) - get the cut-out instance mask that only the camera
//     rays see. MainPsHairAlphaDepthOnly prepass draws count as hair. The sun in full path tracing takes the rewritten
//     t10.x (the game's shadow maps and clouds times the ray-traced contact).
// v23 (user 2026-10-11: GBRT's look - "path tracing + NEE + MIS from RTX Remix", "SHaRC"): rt.hlsl - the hits get NEE of the
//     sun and the game's lamps (the frame's light list from cs_light_census, which now runs before the near-field rays: the
//     game's light loop is switched off and the real counts bound at the start of the pre passes), a spatially hashed
//     radiance cache (SHaRC idea: k_sharc_capacity entries at the end of the data buffer, written by the camera's view in
//     cs_history and by the rays, resolved by cs_sharc_resolve after the history), MIS for the sky lobe. Full path tracing
//     gets the Sun slider (its own sun along the game's direction, ray-traced shadows only) and Brightness.
// v24 (user: sun spots on clothes and every object indoors, light behind the curtains moving): rt.hlsl - the sun's side by
//     the surface's true orientation (from depth; the normal maps' details turned away stored "lit"), the add-on's sun
//     traced per full-resolution pixel in cs_full (now a ray tracing pass), cut-out cards a fixed half transmission.
// v25 (user: GBRT's local lights cast every object's shadow): rt.hlsl cs_lights - lights with a game shadow map are traced
//     too (map x rays; characters' own pixels keep the maps), rays skip the zone before the map's near plane.
// v106 (user, v105: "ok pressed numpad there, the noise is still the same" - rt-recon\inputs-20261015-132142, after a restart:
//     85 lamps remembered, 30 marked hidden; noise 53-61 % a pixel, 3-8 % of the walls exactly black where no game light reaches;
//     the walls lit by about ten comparable lamps - a bulb 1.2 m off, large dim glowing patches 2-7 m off: one or two picks a
//     pixel show one or two of them a frame; the settings: Light size 6 cm (the game's lights' shadow rays barely spread),
//     ReGIR plain slots 100 %, candidates 1, game share 0, ReSTIR DI (RTXPT) Always): rtxpt.hlsl lights the brightest lamps at a
//     camera's surface exactly, as the game's lights since v104 - pt_lamps_top ranks 12 candidates from the cell and the power
//     PDF by their light there before shadows (RTXPT's BSDF x the visibility cache's chance) and keeps "Light samples" (1-8)
//     distinct lamps, pt_lamps_exact gives each its own shadow ray; the rest's light is one NEE sample or ReSTIR DI's pick with
//     those counting for nothing (pt_nee's exclusion set) - unbiased (the ranking's own random numbers). The candidates' two
//     sources apart (pt_draw). "Light samples (RTXPT)" now counts the exact lamps (v101-v105: full NEE samples).
// v105 (user: "go" - v104's captures rt-recon\inputs-20261015-131156 / -131201, "Indirect light only" / "Direct light only":
//     the direct light 20x the indirect on the hall's walls (0.10 / 0.005) and the noise - 40-70 % per pixel, 10 % of the
//     mid wall exactly black; the game's lights exact since v104, so the lamps; of 742 listed lamps 737 not seen for 1600+
//     frames - the streets' and other rooms' lamps, remembered, behind the hall's walls, taking most of the lamps' samples
//     with their shadow rays blocked): rt.hlsl cs_lamp_merge traces one ray from the camera to each listed lamp (short of its
//     own surface) and marks it hidden when blocked (LAMP_HIDDEN, the record's + 44 bit 31 beside v102's dark count);
//     rtxdi_di.hlsl cs_rtxdi_pdf weighs a hidden lamp's power by LAMP_HIDDEN_WEIGHT (0.1) - never 0 (one around a corner still
//     lights what it lights, unbiased), so RTXDI's tiles, the ReGIR cells and RTXPT's NEE spend their samples on the lamps in
//     view or open to it (RTXPT's NEE-AT learns which lights contribute from its rays the same way). lamp_merge is a ray
//     tracing pass now (k_pass_rt); lamps.txt marks the hidden ones.
// v104 (user, v103: "nice, shadows back, but the image is very noisy now"): ReSTIR DI (one reused pick a pixel among the 14
//     game lights and 733 lamps) brought the shadows back with RE9's noise. RTXDI's DI defaults offer nothing for Ray
//     Reconstruction (its PT's do; the boiling filter is not in the fused pass). The visible object shadows are the game's
//     lights' (artist-placed, shadow maps), few per frame: the RTXPT renderer now lights the camera's surfaces with every game
//     light itself (rtxpt.hlsl: each an RTXPT sphere light with its shadow map and its ray every frame, as RE9 v71-v85 and DD2
//     did - crisp, no selection noise; a light under PT_LIGHT_ROULETTE before its shadow takes a roulette for its ray,
//     unbiased) and ReSTIR DI / RTXPT's NEE there take the lamps alone: RTXDI_FRAME_EXACT_GAME (k_rtxdi_flag_exact_game)
//     zeroes a game light's target at a camera's surface (rtxdi_bridge.hlsli), pt_nee's lamps_only draws by power without the
//     game share and drops game candidates. Bounces unchanged. Debug views 4 / 5 hold the lamps / the sun and game lights again.
// v103 (user, v101's captures rt-recon\inputs-20261015-083123 (RTXPT) / -083130 (RE9): "look exactly at the middle wheel at
//     the wall, its casting fine shadowing on re9, it dissappears on rtxpt, notice how rtxpt is much cleaner noise wise, do
//     NOT work with re9 instead, the sharp re9 shadows are still not accomplished"): the hall holds 14 game lights (2 with
//     the game's shadow maps) and 733 listed lamps; RTXPT's NEE takes 2 shadow-tested samples a pixel from 5 candidates, so
//     the light that casts the wheel's shadow comes up in few pixels a frame and Ray Reconstruction averages its shadow out.
//     RE9's crisp shadows were RTXDI's ReSTIR DI: its spatiotemporal reuse settles each pixel on the light that matters there
//     and tests that light's shadow. "ReSTIR DI (RTXPT)" (PTRestirDIMode): Off (RTXPT's NEE), Auto (v99-v102: off while Ray
//     Reconstruction denoises - RTXPT's DisableReSTIRsWithDLSSRR), Always (default) - its pick shaded with RTXPT's BSDF and
//     the light's sphere (Light softness 0: RE9's hard centre shadows). Needs NVIDIA RTXDI lamps.
// v102 (user, v101: "black glitch has been removed on posters, check video capture for the next glitch, lamps seem to be be
//     constantly on an on/off state, once detected, the lamps should stay on, not flickering between on/off"): the video
//     (Videos\Captures 2026-10-15 08-13-38, 557 frames, greenhouses with hanging bulbs and string lights): the light the
//     bulbs throw on the plastic roof and inside switches between two levels for 6-30 frames at a time (roof luma 125 / 76-91,
//     jumps of 30-47 in one frame; frames 113 / 115: the bulbs still glowing, their light gone). The lamps' memory (rt.hlsl
//     cs_lamp_merge) faded a lamp not observed this frame by 0.7 as soon as its centre pixel showed no emission in plain view -
//     a bulb a few pixels wide moved off that pixel by DLSS's jitter, or a lamp left unobserved because a brighter one of its
//     screen cell was observed (one observation per cell) - and an observation brought it back whole. Now it fades only after
//     LAMP_DARK_FRAMES (15) frames in a row of plain view without emission within LAMP_DARK_REACH (2) pixels of its centre (a
//     count in the record's spare word, + 44: lamps.txt "dark"); a lamp that really went out still fades within ~0.5 s.
// v101 (user, v100: "two light samples per frame seem to improve shadow accuracy ... closer to that of re9's low penumbra
//     shadow handling, still far, a slider to get it to re9's shadow accuracy would be ideal to control it, as well as light
//     samples, black fix did not work, ive pressed numpad - with both renderers"): the captures rt-recon\inputs-20261015-074907
//     (RTXPT) / -074915 (RE9): two wall posters pure black in RTXPT (99.5 % of their pixels exactly 0) and lit in RE9; the ray
//     view finds them at the depth buffer's surface, their G-buffer like the wall's (roughness 0.58, not metal, stencil 1).
//     They are cut-out cards: the primary's recast (trace_scene, MASK_SCENE: opaque only) took the wall a few millimetres
//     behind, so all of the path's rays started between the card and the wall and the card's back blocked them (RE9's lamps
//     came from RTXDI's surface at the depth buffer's position). rtxpt.hlsl: a cut-out card within 2 mm + 0.05 % of the depth
//     (pt_trace_cards, solid) is the primary when it lies before the opaque hit. The rest of that capture's black: the bathroom
//     seen through the door (90 % of its pixels 0 a frame) - pure path tracing's dark, noisy indirect light (RE9 took the
//     game's light at its hits), not a defect. Settings (RTXPT): "Light softness (RTXPT)" (PTLightSoftness, 100 %) - how far
//     over a light's sphere its samples and shadow rays spread (0: its centre, hard shadows as RE9's lamps; the light's
//     brightness unchanged; rt.hlsl light_visibility_cone for the game's lights' rays); "Light samples (RTXPT)"
//     (PTLightSamples, 2) - RTXPT's NEEFullSamples at the camera's surfaces, 1-8. g_pass bits 8-14 / 16-19.
// v100 (user, v99: "re9 should not be worked on anymore, rtxpt will be the default one, much better and natural results,
//     however without rtxdi the meshes are now showing the black glitch again"): the Renderer setting's default is RTXPT (a
//     saved PTRendererType still wins); the RE9 renderer is frozen. The glitch: with ReSTIR DI out (NVIDIA RTXDI lamps off,
//     or Ray Reconstruction denoising) the camera's surfaces take RTXPT's NEE, whose weights ignored visibility - v74's
//     blotch (a lamp the weights favour, blocked from there - Ellie's head over it -, picked most frames: its shadow ray
//     black; ReSTIR DI checks visibility as it reuses). rtxpt.hlsl pt_nee: the weights x the chance the light is seen from
//     there (ReGIR's visibility cache, fed by RTXPT's shadow rays too; its floor keeps every light: no bias) - RTXPT's
//     NEE-AT learns its local lights from its shadow rays the same way - and two full samples at the camera's surfaces
//     (RTXPT's NEEFullSamples 2; 1 at the bounces).
// v99 (user, v98: "light handling seems to be the same? only thing changed is how it handles materials"; then "yes i want
//     that, go" - RTXPT's light handling, with physical falloff): the RTXPT renderer (rtxpt.hlsl) lights as RTXPT does.
//     The lights are RTXPT sphere lights (its SphereLight model: uniform over the solid angle the sphere subtends) - each
//     sample toward a point of the sphere, its shadow ray too: the game's lights at their radius (at least Light size's), the
//     lamps at their visible area (their cardioid kept). "Physical light falloff (RTXPT)" (PTPhysicalFalloff, on): the game's
//     lights inverse-square with no range (rt.hlsl light_arrival_ex), each at the intensity that keeps its energy within its
//     authored range (the mean flux out to its cull radius, 8-point Gauss-Legendre: TLOU's 1.11 / (1 + d) - 0.11 lights over
//     9 m -> 1.17 x colour: 2.6x the game's light at 1 m, 0.8x at 3 m, light past 9 m); their cones, cookies, shades, near
//     fade as authored; RTXDI weighs them so too (RTXDI_FRAME_PHYSICAL: its surface target, ReGIR's cells) - the RE9 renderer
//     never sets it. RTXPT's next-event estimation at every vertex (PathTracerNEE.hlsli, derived): 5 candidates (its
//     NEECandidateSamples), 65 % from the local distribution - RTXPT's NEE-AT screen tiles, here the vertex's ReGIR surface
//     cell (regir_cells.hlsli regir_cells_find, split out of regir_cells_pick), its 32 slots as the proxy list - the rest from
//     RTXDI's power PDF with the game lights' share (rtxdi.hlsli regir_source_pdf, split out), weighted reservoir sampling by
//     its EvalSampleWeight (the light's largest channel x the BSDF's pdf), the balance heuristic over both distributions, one
//     shadow-tested sample. At the primary, as RTXPT: ReSTIR DI (cs_rtxdi_di) only while Ray Reconstruction does not denoise
//     (rr_denoising; RTXPT's DisableReSTIRsWithDLSSRR), its pick then re-shaded with RTXPT's BSDF and the light's sphere (v98:
//     RTXDI's own shading); with RR RTXPT's NEE. The game's lights follow RTXDI's list (RTXDI_FRAME_GAME), else v98's loops.
// v98 (user: "rtxpt port to replace dd2 renderer then"; v96's evaluation: "replace the dd2 renderer with the most physically
//     accurate one"): the Renderer setting's second slot is NVIDIA RTXPT's path tracer (src\rtxpt.hlsl; v52-v97 DD2's -
//     dd2.hlsl keeps the lobes reuse.hlsl shades with). RTXPT's shaders (github.com/NVIDIA-RTX/RTXPT Rtxpt\Shaders\PathTracer)
//     unmodified in RTGI_builds\deps\rtxpt (NVIDIA RTX SDKs license: object code only); taken as is: StandardBSDF (Falcor's:
//     Frostbite diffuse, GGX VNDF sampling, height-correlated Smith, delta lobes), ShadingData, its Owen-scrambled Sobol /
//     hash sampling; derived (NVIDIA's notice kept): its reference-mode path loop - a BSDF sample at every vertex, NEE at
