// v59 (user: "ray reconstruction is needed to help reduce noise on dd2"; "go"): DLSS Ray Reconstruction, step 1 - a test. NVIDIA's
//     DLSS SDK 310.9.1 (RTGI_builds\deps\dlss-sdk-310.9.1: headers, nvsdk_ngx_s.lib; nvngx_dlssd.dll 310.9.1 in the game
//     folder). Setting "Ray Reconstruction (test)" (PTRayReconstruction, off): at the lighting list's close NGX starts on the
//     game's device (this add-on's project ID; the game's Streamline runs DLSS Super Resolution on the same device, so NGX is
//     never shut down here - only the feature released), RR's availability is read and its feature created at the render
//     size in DLAA (input = output size: a denoiser before the game's own upscaler). Nothing evaluated yet; the outcome in
//     the settings and ReShade.log (NGX's own lines prefixed "TLOU RT NGX").
// v58 (user's v57 shots: "black splotches are flickering and still a big issue, ellies clothing and face seems to be broken
//     too" - pure black patches in the textures' shapes on clothing, cloth litter and floor decals): the normal maps tilt the
//     shading normal past the viewer or below the triangle. DD2's response gave no light at N.V <= 0, its bounce hemisphere
//     turned into the surface, and the contact ray (both renderers, v45 / v53) counted a direction below the triangle as
//     fully blocked - the indirect light gone for that frame (the flicker). dd2.hlsl dd2_visible_normal lifts the shading
//     normal onto the triangle's side and toward the viewer (the albedos the light is demodulated by keep the G-buffer's);
//     the contact ray mirrors a direction below the triangle above it (re9.hlsl too).
// v57 (user: "shadowing overall is just 3% per log, the games baked lighting just does not generate any path traced direct
//     shadowing, only sunlight"): most of TLOU's light - windows, its static lamps - exists only as baked lighting (t4 flat,
//     t5 lobe toward a dominant direction), and since v51 nothing traced toward it. The DD2 renderer now lights the
//     primary from the lobe as a light in that direction with one shadow ray in the Baked shadow softness cone (slider back,
//     PTShadowSoftness) up to Ray length, under the game's small-scale occlusion; the first bounce's escapes take the flat
//     part alone. Its bounce rays reach Ray length (DD2: 1000 m) - beyond it the baked lighting from that direction stands
//     for the light there (v52-v56: walls everywhere indoors, no baked light reached an indoor pixel).
// v56 (user: "still no shadows, only from sunlight, lamps slider does nothing, indoors areas get extremely dark even with a
//     clear light source"): the DD2 renderer left out the add-on's emitters (the game's emissive surfaces on screen as
//     lights - its static lamps exist only as emission and baked light): now at the primary and at every bounce hit, RIS
//     over the list with one shadow ray (rt.hlsl emitter_irradiance), / the lighting's factor as RE9's ReSTIR DI - Lamps
//     works there and lamps cast shadows. Its escaped paths took the game's sky visibility (t10.y) on the baked ambient
//     too (v53), which already holds the room's occlusion: indoors the rays out through windows and gaps brought next to
//     nothing - now only the open sky's share (Baked light below 1) takes it (dd2.hlsl dd2_escaped).
// v55 (user, v54: "black splotches still there, look at clothing and meshes" - black shapes beside the papers on a floor, on
//     clothing): the rays' cut-out masks (v30: the depth shader's first texture, channel x, at the stream-out coordinates,
//     cutoff 0.25) model only the simple alpha tests - 252 of the game's alpha-tested depth shaders, many blending up to 14
//     samples with constant-buffer coordinate maths. A wrong mask blocked the light where the card is not drawn (in the
//     DD2 renderer the bounce paths under it too: pure black). cs_motion's camera rays now vote, one pixel in four, on each
//     cut-out card they meet: at the depth buffer's surface the game drew it (solid), clearly in front of it the game
//     discarded it (a hole); rt.hlsl mask_distrusted lets the light through a draw whose mask disagrees on more than 35%
//     of 24 votes or more this frame (Geo: k_mask_vote_base, 8 bytes per listed draw, cleared by cs_mask; late and
//     retained draws do not vote). Status line 2: the votes and the share disagreeing.
// v54 (user's v53 shots: "black splotches, and these lines when sun shadows are on" - black lines beside tyre tracks in snow,
//     black shapes by litter on a floor; "the eye fix creates this flickering blocky pattern on sky"): 41 of the game's
//     depth-pass pixel shaders (MainPsDepthOnlyCustomDepth*) write SV_DepthGreaterEqual - parallax relief (snow ruts,
//     decals) whose depth lies behind its triangle - and sample a height map, so the add-on took them for cut-outs with
//     that map as the mask: the visible surface sat under its own triangle and the rays were blocked in the map's pattern.
//     They are solid now (kind 0, in the scene the recast traces), and the RE9 / DD2 recast looks 0.3 m + 5% of the
//     distance in front of the depth buffer's surface (re9.hlsl RE9_RECAST_FRONT) so such a pixel starts on its
//     triangle. The forward relight leaves the sky alone (cs_re9_ratio: RELIGHT_SKY, not in the tile sums) - the game
//     draws its sky after the composite, and v50-v53 scaled it in 16-pixel blocks by the tiles' ratio.
// v53 (user, v52's DD2 renderer: "very bright, seems to blur all details", "no shadowing on anything else, only bounce and
//     occlusion"): dd2.hlsl stores the indirect light demodulated (the primary's sample weight split into diffuse /
//     specular, RE9_RAW_D / RE9_RAW_S; the composite multiplies by Ad / As again) - v52 accumulated radiance with the
//     textures in it and the reprojection blurred them; the contact ray, the game's small-scale occlusion and its sky
//     visibility on escaped paths as the RE9 renderer (Occlusion, Contact shadows); the game's sun at a bounce hit the
//     camera sees takes the game's shadow mask there (v52: the ray alone, through the culled roofs and walls). rt.hlsl
//     seen_light (the RE9 renderer's hits: what the camera saw) reads the renderers' full-resolution RE9_GEO plane - its
//     PLANE_GEO lost its writer (cs_temporal) in v51 and nothing was found as seen in v51-v52.
// v52 (v51's request, second half): the DD2 renderer (src\dd2.hlsl), Dragon's Dogma 2's Pure Path Tracing as decoded from
//     REFramework's capture (RTGI_builds\dd2-pt-analysis\pt_algorithm.txt): the primary from the G-buffer, re-traced like
//     RE9's; every light of the frame with its own shadow ray x its shadow map, the sun with one jittered ray, DD2's Hammon
//     diffuse + GGX (Smith joint approx) with the split-sum LUT's multiple-scattering compensation; Bounces x (one 50/50
//     Lambert / GGX spherical-cap VNDF sample over the mixture's pdf, the bounce ray with alpha-tested cut-outs, the hit's
//     material from the material cache with the roughness never decreasing along the path, NEE of the sun and one light
//     picked uniformly x the count); a miss takes the sky and ends the path. No Russian roulette, no reservoirs; white
//     noise for DD2's blue-noise Sobol. cs_re9_accum accumulates its indirect light (DD2 leaves the noise to TAA / DLSS),
//     cs_dd2_composite writes the light buffer; the history and the forward relight as RE9's. Settings "Renderer"
//     (PTRendererType: RE9 / DD2) and "Bounces (DD2)" (PTDD2Bounces, 3); "Baked light" (PTBaked) back - v51 dropped the
//     slider while both renderers' escaped rays still read it.
// v51 (user: "port the entire dragons dogma 2 path tracer, as a separate toggle, scrap tlou2's original path tracer, its
//     worse. keep re9 and dragons dogma 2 only"): TLOU RT's own path tracer is gone - before the lighting the half-resolution
//     near-field rays (cs_trace_rt), their ReSTIR GI (cs_restir) / PT (cs_pt), the accumulation and filter (cs_temporal,
//     cs_atrous) and the rewritten ambient, sun mask and reflections copied into the lighting's inputs (cs_ambient); after
//     it the v19-v43 full path tracing (cs_full) and its history (cs_history). The game's lighting runs on its own inputs
//     (the RE9 renderer's escaped rays read the game's baked ambient again, not the add-on's; the forward relight compares
//     with the game's own result); the RE9 renderer is the path tracer (Dragon's Dogma 2's next, v52). Kept: the scene
//     (stream-out, BLAS / TLAS, masks, motion), the material cache, the sky, SHaRC, the emitters, the game's lights with
//     ray-traced shadows. Settings gone: RE9 renderer (always), ReSTIR DI (always), ReSTIR PT, Reflections, Shadow
//     softness, Baked light. The add-on runs while Path tracing or Light shadows is above 0.
// v50 (user: "go" - the eyes glowing in the dark): the forward relight. After CS_DeferredLighting the game draws forward
//     layers into the light buffer (RT Recon frame map: 4 MainPsCheapForward draws - the eyes - and a MainPsStandard draw),
//     lit by its own probes and lights; then CS_ApplyFogDirectlyToFullRes runs on it (u0). Right before that fog dispatch
//     the add-on copies the light buffer into the data buffer, runs src\relight.hlsl on the copy (its own root signature: a
//     root UAV and 8 constants) and copies it back, the game's compute state restored: where the layers changed a pixel
//     from what the RE9 renderer left (P), the change is scaled by the path tracing's change of the lighting around - the
//     luminance of P over the game's own result G over 3 x 3 tiles of 16 px (cs_re9_ratio after the composite). Once a frame,
//     only with this frame's composite (frame token). Setting "Relight forward layers" (PTRelight, on).
// v49 (user, v47 session: "occlusion slider does not anything, light shadows slider doesnt do anything aswell, seems like no
//     shadows get rendered", "eyes still glowing at dark"): the RE9 renderer's occlusion of the indirect light (the game's
//     small-scale occlusion x the contact ray) now follows the Occlusion slider - it reached only the escaped sky's game
//     visibility before. (Light shadows scales the rays on top of the lights' own shadow maps, which this frame's lights
//     have; the eyes are drawn by the game's MainPsCheapForward draws into the light buffer after the lighting.)
// v48 (user: "restir gi 2. is next"): ReSTIR GI in the RE9 renderer as RE9 runs it - a diffuse and a specular reservoir per
//     full-resolution pixel (16 bytes: W, distance and M, direction, the light), this frame's path the initial candidate,
//     cs_re9_gi_temporal merging last frame's at the reprojected pixel (M capped at 20), cs_re9_gi_spatial 4 neighbours on
//     a 32-pixel disk (their sample point reconnected from here) and resolving - specular blended with the raw sample by
//     min(alpha, 0.6). Beyond RE9: a neighbour's pick is traced for visibility (this scene lacks what the game culled). The
//     path's sample now feeds both lobes over the mixture's pdf (RE9's one-sample MIS). Setting "ReSTIR GI" (PTRestirGI)
//     drives it too. Four reservoir planes after the RE9 planes.
// v47 (user: "the skin subsurface seems broken too"): the RE9 renderer added the game's subsurface light (t7: CS_SkinDiffuse
//     - the skin's diffuse light from the game's own baked ambient, sun shadow maps and lights - after its SSSS blur) on
//     top of its own full diffuse light, where the game fades its diffuse by the material's subsurface amount (G-buffer 1
//     .x bits 0-7 under material flag t3 bit 3) and lets t7 bring it: the skin's diffuse twice, half of it lit by the game.
//     Now the skin's own diffuse light (ReSTIR DI's and the game sun's diffuse part kept apart, plus the indirect) goes
//     through the game's separable kernel (CS_SsssSepHor/Vert's ssssSepKernel, 25 taps, depth-aware): cs_re9_sss the
//     horizontal pass, the composite the vertical; t7 is no longer added. Three more planes (RE9_PLANES 13).
// v46 (user, v45 shots: "ellies hair and eyes are broken, along some other objects, they glow in the dark, and in bright
//     areas they get dark"): cs_motion flagged a pixel as hair wherever a hair draw's card lay within 1% of the depth
//     buffer's surface - the whole quad, its ray being opaque - so the skin and the eyes under eyelash and strand cards kept
//     the game's lighting: lit rectangles over the eyes, skin-coloured patches by the ear. The card's own mask now decides
//     (cutout_transmission, as the shadow rays test it). And the RE9 renderer path traces hair too (two-sided: the normal
//     toward the viewer; no recast, the scene's rays leave hair out; ReSTIR DI's reservoirs there): the game's lighting
//     follows its baked light, not this renderer's, so hair glowed in dark rooms and went black in the sun.
// v45 (user, v44: "seems to lack shadows and overall occlusion of scene, indoors areas look brightly lit"): the RE9
//     renderer had no sun at all with Sun 0 (the game's sun: ReSTIR DI samples only the add-on's) - now the game's sun with
//     its shadow mask as cs_full; and the occlusion RE9 needs no term for (its scene is complete, this one lacks what the
//     game culled and the fine detail): the game's small-scale occlusion and the contact ray on the indirect light, the
//     game's sky visibility (t10.y) on the sky reached through the scene, by Occlusion.
// v44 (user: "a complete port of re9, rewrite everything from tlou2" - keeping the scene capture, Ray Reconstruction as the
//     denoiser to come): the RE9 renderer (src\re9.hlsl), Resident Evil Requiem's path tracer as reconstructed from its
//     shaders (re9-pt-analysis): full render resolution, one path per pixel; the camera ray's end re-traced against the
//     BVH; direct light from ReSTIR DI; one BSDF sample, the lobe picked by the lobes' albedos, GGX visible-normal
//     sampling; diffuse and specular kept apart, demodulated by their albedos; a reprojected temporal accumulation per lobe
//     stands in for Ray Reconstruction (v45); the composite replaces cs_full, cs_re9_history cs_history. Setting
//     "RE9 renderer" (PTRenderer, on; off = the v19-v43 full path tracing).
// v43 (user: "restir pt"): ReSTIR PT on the half-resolution paths (rt.hlsl): the cosine rays' paths - through a hit or
//     to the sky - resampled into one path reservoir per pixel (64 bytes, two planes after ReSTIR DI's), moved between
//     pixels by a hybrid shift (reconnection at a hit far enough from both primaries, random replay of closer ones, the
//     direction of sky paths; visibility rays), temporal and spatial reuse (3 neighbours) weighted by pairwise MIS with
//     confidence weights; cs_pt writes B and E. Setting "ReSTIR PT" (PTRestirPT, on; in place of ReSTIR GI while on).
// v42 (user: "first start restir di" - the way to ReSTIR PT): ReSTIR DI for full path tracing's direct light (rt.hlsl):
//     one reservoir per full-resolution pixel (48 bytes, two planes after the emitters' list) holding one light sample -
//     the add-on's sun (a direction in its cone), a game light of the pixel's tile, or an emitter. cs_di (after the game's
//     lights' passes): RIS over the sun, 2 tile lights, 2 emitters (target: the unshadowed light with the game's BRDF and
//     the lights' own maps), temporal reuse at the reprojected pixel (history 20); cs_full: 3 neighbours (spatial reuse),
//     one shadow ray, f V W in place of the sun term, the tile lights' sum and v39's emitter RIS. Setting "ReSTIR DI"
//     (PTRestirDI, on). Hair keeps the game's lighting.
// v41 (user: "contact shadows are there, but they look blurry"): rt.hlsl cs_full - the sky's directional shadow traced
//     per full-resolution pixel (one ray toward the ambient's dominant direction in the Shadow softness cone, like the
//     sun's), sharpening the filtered half-resolution sky light by the ray's visibility over S on the lobe's share; contact
//     rays 0.5 m with a squared falloff (dark only near the occluder).
// v40 (user: "my logs show just one light, bounce lighting does not generate shadows like gbrt"; v39's log: the light
//     buffer before the lighting holds light on 34-100% of the screen - not emission; the 34% frames passed the gate and
//     listed ~390 false lamps, at the user's Lamps 8): the gate is now 5% of the screen. The bounce and sky light lost their
//     shadows in the filter (5 a-trous passes, steps up to 16 half-resolution pixels): 3 passes now (ReSTIR GI's samples
//     are steadier), and cs_full traces one contact ray per full-resolution pixel (1 m) that shadows the bounce and sky light
//     under and between objects. Slider "Contact shadows" (PTContact, 1).
// v39 (user: "go" - the game lights after ReSTIR GI; only Ellie's flashlight is a game light in most scenes): the
//     emissive surfaces as lights. The light buffer holds their own light before CS_DeferredLighting (rt.hlsl cs_emit keeps
//     it per pixel, gated off when half the screen or more holds light: then it is not emission); cs_emit_list makes up to
//     1024 point emitters from a 32 x 32 screen grid; cs_full lights every surface from them (RIS of 4, one shadow ray: lamp
//     shadows) and puts the emitters' own light back (v19-v38 full path tracing dropped it); the history leaves it out of
//     the bounce rays. Slider "Lamps" (PTLamps, 1). Status line 6: emissive pixels, emitters listed.
// v38 (user: "go with restir gi first"; v37's capture: the slabs gone, the shadows still unsteady): ReSTIR GI on the
//     bounce light (rt.hlsl): the cosine rays' hits are resampled into one reservoir per half-resolution pixel (target
//     luminance x cosine), merged with last frame's at the reprojected pixel (history capped at 16) and with 4 neighbours'
//     (a ray checks a neighbour's sample is in view), Jacobian-corrected; cs_restir writes the bounce light B from it.
//     Two reservoir planes (48 bytes per half-resolution pixel) after SHaRC. Setting "ReSTIR GI" (PTRestirGI, on).
// v37 (user's v36 capture: in one frame of three the snow under the bushes is covered by the whole triangles of their
//     cards' shadows, gone the next): the game refills its shader-visible heap from staging descriptors every frame, and
//     the masks of late draws (the frame before's) and retained ones were read through their old slots - by then often
//     another texture. Each draw now records its mask's texture identity (the staging descriptor last copied into its slot,
//     from the descriptor tracking: table entry + 40); cs_convert lists the slots of the cut-outs drawn on time this frame
//     by identity, and rt.hlsl cs_mask gives every late or retained cut-out the slot of its texture this frame, or lets
//     the light through when none names it. Status line 2: how many were remapped / left without a mask.
// v36 (user: Baked light makes no difference, the shadows still flicker; v35's log: the draws per frame swing between
//     about half and one and a half times the median, min + max = 2 x median - draws moving between neighbouring frames):
//     the game records some of a frame's depth-pass draws after its lighting took the epoch; the GPU runs them before that
//     lighting and they join the next epoch, where cs_convert placed them with that next frame's camera - a copy of the
//     surface shifted by the camera's motion and jitter, casting shadows that came and went with the recording order. A
//     pass after each conversion (rt.hlsl cs_late) now marks the next epoch's draws whose stream-out counters show them
//     run, and the next cs_convert places those with the frame before's camera. Status line 2: a frame's late draws;
//     line 4: the queues the depth pass and the lighting are submitted to.
// v35 (user: "the sky probe for proper GBRT lighting"; v28's log: the game has no distant probe): the add-on's own sky
//     (rt.hlsl cs_sky, after the motion pass): the game's outdoor ambient on open, upward-facing pixels (t10.y near 1), kept
//     over the frames - the rays that leave the scene take it as Baked light goes to 0. Status line 5: its luminance, the
//     open pixels this frame, its age.
// v34 (user: v33 did not help; the v32 capture frame by frame: in single frames the snow is covered by huge flat
//     polygons - draws of another view the game renders every few frames with the same depth shaders, converted with the
//     main camera's matrices): a depth-pass draw counts only when its target is the main view's size (the size with the
//     most depth-pass draws, the render size when it has any - learnt per epoch, s_main_view); the others are left out and
//     counted. The BLAS budget (768 MB) was full of those draws' meshes: when an allocation fails, entries unused for 8
//     frames are evicted at the next build (else 300).
// v33 (user's capture: leaf shadows on the snow come and go between frames, Ellie's hair looks wrong; v32's log: 39-308
//     draws per frame in one spot, the collapse guard taking the last scene up to 8 times in 30 frames): the game records
//     some of a frame's draws after its lighting took the epoch; they come one frame late, and a draw whose timing changes
//     is missing from a frame. Now the last scene's draws missing from this one are kept for one frame (retained: their
//     BLAS, vertices and table entry as last frame - table flags bit 3, cs_convert neither converts nor checks them), with
//     three regions (epoch % 3) so last frame's vertices stay intact while the late draws already stream into the next.
//     The collapse guard is gone (v29's reuse stays for empty or stale epochs), and a reused frame keeps the motion plane's
//     pixel flags (v26-v32 switched them off with the motion: hair got the full path tracing in those frames).
// v32 (v31's log: 100% of the mask tests "counter short"): the rays read the coordinates' counter after cs_zero had
//     cleared it (v27 zeroes each region right after the conversion); cs_convert now checks it and marks the instance
//     (InstanceContributionToHitGroupIndex bit 0), which the rays read with their candidate.
// v31 (user's v30 screenshot: the cards' shadows still half-transparent slabs, while the log shows every cut-out draw with
//     its mask): counters for why cutout_transmission fell back (status line 2) and for the lists whose heap check failed.
// v30 (user: plants' and Ellie's hair shadows are slabs; "OMM"): the cut-outs' own masks in the rays. A cut-out's depth
//     pass (an alpha-tested pixel shader) is read at pipeline creation for the mask it tests (alpha_inputs: its first
//     TEXCOORD input read in x and y, its first material texture - 68 of the 75 alpha-tested depth shaders of the v2
//     capture test that texture's first channel); its stream-out clone writes those coordinates to a second slot
//     (k_uv_base regions), each draw records the texture's slot in the game's descriptor heap, and the rays test the mask
//     texel at the hit (rt.hlsl cutout_transmission: holes let the light through, leaves block it) - what an opacity
//     micromap bakes, per texel. The heap is the add-on's unbounded table HeapTextures (space 102), bound at the heap's
//     start only when the lighting's tables are in the same heap (g_alpha_on). The draw table grows to 48 bytes per draw.
// v29 (user: Path tracing 0 removes every flash; v28's capture: single frames with the game's own lighting - about twice
//     as bright at Brightness 0.53, the game's stronger sun on the snow with the same shadow shapes): a frame whose epoch
//     holds no geometry (empty, or begun more than 2 presents ago) was skipped whole - no passes, no full path tracing -
//     and showed the game's lighting. It now reuses the last scene like a collapsed one (v26). Every skip of the passes
//     before and after the lighting is counted by reason and logged whenever one happens (ReShade.log, "anomalies").
// v28 (user: still flickering, GBRT shadows the whole scene; v27's log: no draw incomplete now, but up to 16000 draws in
//     20 s not streamed for lack of space): the regions doubled (288 MB each) and the largest draw left out is reported
//     (status line 3); the Baked light slider: the share of the game's baked ambient in the rays that leave the near field
//     (0 = GBRT-style: the open sky from the game's distant probe, the baked sky shadow and ambient lobe gone - the light
//     has to arrive through the scene), how often the sky probe was found in status line 5.
// v27 (user: still flickering; v26's log: 4-26% of each scene's draws "incomplete", a different set every frame): a draw
//     the game records after the frame's lighting took the epoch belongs to the next epoch (the other region), but the GPU
//     runs it before that lighting - whose zero pass (v7-v26: the other region's counters, before the conversion) then
//     wiped its stream-out counter, and the next frame dropped it as incomplete. Now each lighting zeroes its own region's
//     counters after its conversion read them (cs_zero after cs_convert): a late draw's counter survives to its epoch.
// v26 (user's capture: whole frames lose every ray-traced shadow for one frame, every 4-8 frames): the frame's scene came
//     from a near-empty epoch. A lighting pass whose t4 is not at render size no longer takes the epoch (pre_lighting);
//     an epoch under half the last built one's draws reuses the last scene (TLAS, BLASes and table kept; once; no motion
//     that frame); scene statistics in status line 4, and all status lines in ReShade.log every ~20 s.

